        "title": "Epic and Lyric",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|              EPIC AND LYRIC — LANDSCAPE                          |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  EPIC                              LYRIC                         |\n|  ----                              -----                         |\n|  Narrative of heroic action        Expression of single voice/   |\n|  Communal, public, monumental      mood; private, intimate       |\n|  Long                              Short to medium               |\n|  Oral origins → written            Often musical origins         |\n|                                                                  |\n|  ORAL-FORMULAIC TRADITION          FIXED LYRIC FORMS             |\n|  Parry (1928) + Lord (1960)        Sonnet (Italian → English)    |\n|  → composition in performance     Ode (Pindar → Horace → Keats) |\n|  → formulae as generative units   Elegy, epigram, ghazal         |\n|  → the Homeric Question resolved  Villanelle, sestina, triolet   |\n|                                                                  |\n|  EPIC TRADITION                    LYRIC TRADITION               |\n|  Iliad, Odyssey (Greek)            Sappho, Pindar (Greek)        |\n|  Aeneid (Latin)                    Horace, Catullus (Latin)      |\n|  Mahabharata, Ramayana (Sanskrit)  Sangam poems (Tamil)          |\n|  Sundiata (West African)           Tang shi (Chinese)            |\n|  Beowulf, Song of Roland           Troubadour canso, alba        |\n|  Paradise Lost (literary epic)     Ghazal (Persian/Urdu)         |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
